1
00:03:50,773 --> 00:03:51,315
Ah.

2
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:55,653
Ah...

3
00:05:31,582 --> 00:05:32,833
Get Charlie.

4
00:05:43,928 --> 00:05:45,596
Dr. Holloway!

5
00:05:48,474 --> 00:05:49,808
Charlie!

6
00:05:58,692 --> 00:05:59,443
What?

7
00:06:00,194 --> 00:06:02,029
Come quick!

8
00:06:31,850 --> 00:06:34,061
- Did you date it?
- Thirty-five thousand...

9
00:06:34,228 --> 00:06:36,272
...years. Maybe older.

10
00:06:38,816 --> 00:06:40,234
Hm.

11
00:07:00,004 --> 00:07:02,381
You gotta be kidding me.

12
00:07:05,259 --> 00:07:07,011
It's the same configuration.

13
00:07:08,804 --> 00:07:12,099
I mean, it's gotta predate
the others by millennia.

14
00:07:25,863 --> 00:07:28,157
I think they want us
to come and find them.

15
00:07:30,868 --> 00:07:31,952
Yeah.

16
00:08:37,893 --> 00:08:40,437
What happened to that man?

17
00:08:40,604 --> 00:08:41,689
He died.

18
00:08:43,148 --> 00:08:44,566
Why aren't you helping them?

19
00:08:45,859 --> 00:08:47,569
They don't want my help.

20
00:08:47,736 --> 00:08:49,279
Their God is different than ours.

21
00:08:51,615 --> 00:08:53,033
Why did he die?

22
00:08:53,951 --> 00:08:55,869
Because sooner or later, everyone does.

23
00:08:56,036 --> 00:08:57,287
Like Mummy?

24
00:08:59,289 --> 00:09:00,541
Like Mummy.

25
00:09:02,292 --> 00:09:03,919
Where do they go?

26
00:09:05,462 --> 00:09:07,548
Everyone has their own word.

27
00:09:07,798 --> 00:09:08,966
Heaven.

28
00:09:09,633 --> 00:09:10,467
Paradise.

29
00:09:12,302 --> 00:09:14,596
Whatever it's called,
it's someplace beautiful.

30
00:09:14,763 --> 00:09:16,432
How do you know it's beautiful?

31
00:09:18,475 --> 00:09:19,977
That's what I choose to believe.

32
00:09:20,310 --> 00:09:21,937
What do you believe, Ellie?

33
00:09:56,305 --> 00:09:58,098
<i>Good morning, David.</i>

34
00:09:59,183 --> 00:10:00,809
<i>Transmitting message.</i>

35
00:10:03,854 --> 00:10:05,689
<i>No response.</i>

36
00:10:15,407 --> 00:10:16,867
<i>Whilst this articulation...</i>

37
00:10:17,034 --> 00:10:19,787
<i>...is attested in the Indo-European descendants...</i>

38
00:10:19,953 --> 00:10:21,830
<i>...as a purely paralinguistic form...</i>

39
00:10:21,997 --> 00:10:25,083
<i>...it is phonemic in the ancestral
form dating back...</i>

40
00:10:25,250 --> 00:10:27,211
<i>...five millennia or more.</i>

41
00:10:27,878 --> 00:10:29,379
<i>Let's attempt Schleicher's fable.</i>

42
00:10:29,880 --> 00:10:30,672
<i>Repeat after me.</i>

43
00:10:44,061 --> 00:10:45,187
<i>Perfect.</i>

44
00:10:45,771 --> 00:10:48,065
- <i>Mr. Lawrence?</i>
- <i>Yes?</i>

45
00:10:48,565 --> 00:10:50,484
- <i>Flimsy, sir.</i>
- <i>Thank you.</i>

46
00:10:57,533 --> 00:11:00,160
<i>You do that once too often.
It's only flesh and blood!</i>

47
00:11:00,911 --> 00:11:03,413
<i>Michael George Hartley,
you're a philosopher.</i>

48
00:11:03,956 --> 00:11:05,249
<i>And you're barmy.</i>

49
00:11:09,419 --> 00:11:10,546
<i>Ooh!</i>

50
00:11:10,712 --> 00:11:13,090
- <i>It damn well hurts!</i>
- <i>Certainly, it hurts.</i>

51
00:11:14,216 --> 00:11:15,717
<i>Well, what's the trick, then?</i>

52
00:11:16,593 --> 00:11:19,680
<i>The trick, William Potter,
is not minding that it hurts.</i>

53
00:11:20,138 --> 00:11:23,433
The trick, William Potter...

54
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:26,687
...is not minding that it hurts.

55
00:11:32,234 --> 00:11:35,237
The trick, William Potter...

56
00:11:35,821 --> 00:11:39,324
...is not minding that it hurts.

57
00:11:52,963 --> 00:11:54,631
<i>Attention:</i>

58
00:11:54,798 --> 00:11:56,216
<i>Destination threshold.</i>

59
00:11:56,633 --> 00:11:57,718
<i>Attention:</i>

60
00:11:57,885 --> 00:11:59,887
<i>Destination threshold.</i>

61
00:12:00,345 --> 00:12:02,848
<i>Attention: Destination threshold.</i>

62
00:13:05,160 --> 00:13:06,203
Robe.

63
00:13:17,673 --> 00:13:18,966
How long?

64
00:13:19,216 --> 00:13:21,510
Two years, four months, 18 days...

65
00:13:21,677 --> 00:13:22,886
...36 hours, 15 minutes.

66
00:13:23,220 --> 00:13:24,471
Any casualties?

67
00:13:25,013 --> 00:13:26,139
Casualties, ma'am?

68
00:13:26,306 --> 00:13:27,391
Has anyone died?

69
00:13:27,933 --> 00:13:29,851
No, ma'am. Everyone is fine.

70
00:13:31,937 --> 00:13:33,480
Well, then wake them up.

71
00:13:44,783 --> 00:13:46,702
Try to relax, Dr. Shaw.

72
00:13:47,911 --> 00:13:49,496
My name is David.

73
00:13:50,872 --> 00:13:53,250
Your mind and body are
in a state of shock...

74
00:13:53,417 --> 00:13:55,502
...as a result of the stasis.

75
00:13:55,752 --> 00:13:57,629
It's all right. Perfectly normal.

76
00:13:57,838 --> 00:13:58,588
Ellie.

77
00:13:59,881 --> 00:14:01,258
We're here, baby.

78
00:14:05,595 --> 00:14:06,430
There, there.

79
00:14:08,265 --> 00:14:10,475
<i>Drink plenty of water.
Drink plenty of fluids.</i>

80
00:14:12,019 --> 00:14:13,437
<i>Hydration aids muscle mass.</i>

81
00:14:18,608 --> 00:14:21,695
<i>All crew, consume shakes
with high caloric content.</i>

82
00:14:27,951 --> 00:14:30,120
What the hell is that?

83
00:14:32,205 --> 00:14:33,081
It's Christmas.

84
00:14:33,498 --> 00:14:34,624
We need the holidays...

85
00:14:34,791 --> 00:14:36,334
...to show time is still moving.

86
00:14:36,501 --> 00:14:37,419
Mission briefing...

87
00:14:37,586 --> 00:14:39,129
...is about to start, captain.

88
00:14:39,629 --> 00:14:41,214
Might wanna make your way down.

89
00:14:41,465 --> 00:14:43,800
Well, I haven't had any breakfast yet.

90
00:14:44,301 --> 00:14:45,719
Is this seat taken?

91
00:14:46,803 --> 00:14:49,431
Hi, I'm Millburn, Biology.
Nice to meet you.

92
00:14:51,641 --> 00:14:52,392
Okay.

93
00:14:52,559 --> 00:14:56,063
Look, um, no offense, but, uh...

94
00:14:56,605 --> 00:14:58,648
...I've been asleep two years.

95
00:14:59,316 --> 00:15:01,860
I ain't here to be your friend.

96
00:15:02,152 --> 00:15:03,487
I'm here to make money.

97
00:15:04,613 --> 00:15:05,947
You got that?

98
00:15:08,492 --> 00:15:09,659
Okay.

99
00:15:10,160 --> 00:15:12,871
I bet a hundred credits
it's a terraforming survey.

100
00:15:13,038 --> 00:15:14,998
No, if it's a survey, they would tell us.

101
00:15:15,165 --> 00:15:17,417
It's a corporate run,
they're not telling us shit.

102
00:15:17,584 --> 00:15:18,418
Come on.

103
00:15:18,585 --> 00:15:20,504
- A hundred.
- All right, you're on.

104
00:15:25,175 --> 00:15:26,593
You look nervous, El.

105
00:15:27,177 --> 00:15:30,013
- I'll try to keep my feet on the ground.
- I know you will.

106
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:31,098
Good morning.

107
00:15:31,848 --> 00:15:34,392
For those of you I hired personally...

108
00:15:35,393 --> 00:15:37,521
...it's nice to see you again.

109
00:15:38,313 --> 00:15:42,192
For the rest of you, I am Meredith Vickers.
It's my job to make sure you do yours.

110
00:15:42,692 --> 00:15:45,278
Okay, then. On with the show.

111
00:15:50,867 --> 00:15:52,619
<i>Weyland Corporation.</i>

112
00:15:52,869 --> 00:15:54,871
<i>Building better worlds.</i>

113
00:15:56,164 --> 00:15:57,749
Hello, friends.

114
00:15:59,709 --> 00:16:01,461
My name is Peter Weyland.

115
00:16:03,505 --> 00:16:05,298
I am your employer.

116
00:16:05,549 --> 00:16:10,595
I am recording this, 22 June, 2091.
And if you're watching it...

117
00:16:11,388 --> 00:16:13,557
...you have reached your destination.

118
00:16:14,224 --> 00:16:16,226
And I am long dead.

119
00:16:16,852 --> 00:16:18,812
May I rest in peace.

120
00:16:20,021 --> 00:16:23,191
There's a man sitting with you today.
His name is David.

121
00:16:23,358 --> 00:16:26,403
And he is the closest thing
to a son I will ever have.

122
00:16:26,695 --> 00:16:29,447
Unfortunately, he is not human.

123
00:16:29,739 --> 00:16:30,991
He will never grow old...

124
00:16:31,241 --> 00:16:33,118
...and he will never die.

125
00:16:34,077 --> 00:16:37,247
And yet he is unable to appreciate
these remarkable gifts...

126
00:16:37,414 --> 00:16:41,751
...for that would require the one thing
that David will never have.

127
00:16:41,918 --> 00:16:43,086
A soul.

128
00:16:44,087 --> 00:16:45,839
I have spent my entire...

129
00:16:46,006 --> 00:16:48,466
...lifetime contemplating the questions:

130
00:16:48,758 --> 00:16:50,260
Where do we come from?

131
00:16:50,594 --> 00:16:52,053
What is our purpose?

132
00:16:52,345 --> 00:16:53,763
What happens when we die?

133
00:16:54,472 --> 00:16:58,393
And I have found two people who convinced
me they're on the verge of answering them.

134
00:16:59,102 --> 00:17:02,063
Drs. Holloway and Shaw,
if you would please stand.

135
00:17:04,357 --> 00:17:06,443
As far as you're concerned,
they're in charge.

136
00:17:07,569 --> 00:17:12,157
The Titan Prometheus wanted to give
mankind equal footing with the gods...

137
00:17:12,324 --> 00:17:15,911
...and for that, he was cast from Olympus.

138
00:17:16,786 --> 00:17:21,625
Well, my friends, the time has
finally come for his return.

139
00:17:22,250 --> 00:17:23,210
Doctors, please.

140
00:17:27,547 --> 00:17:28,465
The floor is yours.

141
00:17:36,973 --> 00:17:38,975
Okay, uh, wow.

142
00:17:39,142 --> 00:17:40,518
All right.

143
00:17:40,685 --> 00:17:42,479
I never had to follow a ghost before.

144
00:17:42,854 --> 00:17:44,898
Okay, let me show you why you guys...

145
00:17:45,065 --> 00:17:46,191
...are here.

146
00:17:53,657 --> 00:17:58,828
These are images of archeological
digs from all over the Earth.

147
00:17:58,995 --> 00:17:59,829
That's...

148
00:17:59,996 --> 00:18:03,333
...Egyptian, Mayan, Sumerian, Babylonian.

149
00:18:03,625 --> 00:18:06,419
That's Hawaiian there at the end,
then Mesopotamian.

150
00:18:06,670 --> 00:18:09,673
Now, this one here is our
most recent discovery.

151
00:18:09,839 --> 00:18:13,843
It's a 35,000-year-old cave painting
from the Isle of Skye in Scotland.

152
00:18:14,135 --> 00:18:18,348
These are ancient civilizations.
They were separated by centuries.

153
00:18:18,515 --> 00:18:21,601
They shared no contact
with one another, and yet...

154
00:18:25,438 --> 00:18:26,523
The same pictogram...

155
00:18:26,690 --> 00:18:30,318
...showing men worshiping giant
beings, pointing to the stars...

156
00:18:30,527 --> 00:18:33,488
...was discovered at every last one of them.

157
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:36,658
And the only galactic system that matched...

158
00:18:37,701 --> 00:18:40,245
...was so far from Earth that there's no way...

159
00:18:40,412 --> 00:18:44,541
...that these primitive, ancient civilizations
could have possibly known about it.

160
00:18:44,708 --> 00:18:46,209
But it just so happens...

161
00:18:47,919 --> 00:18:49,254
...that that system...

162
00:18:50,714 --> 00:18:51,840
...has a sun...

163
00:18:52,882 --> 00:18:54,301
...a lot like ours.

164
00:18:55,343 --> 00:18:59,681
And, based on our long-range scans,
there seemed to be a planet.

165
00:19:01,099 --> 00:19:03,393
Just one planet with a moon...

166
00:19:03,560 --> 00:19:05,437
...capable of sustaining life.

167
00:19:06,896 --> 00:19:08,982
And we arrived there this morning.

168
00:19:11,234 --> 00:19:16,072
So you're saying we're here because of a map
you two kids found in a cave. Is that right?

169
00:19:17,365 --> 00:19:18,658
- No.
- Yeah.

170
00:19:20,160 --> 00:19:23,371
- Um...
- No. Not a map, an invitation.

171
00:19:23,538 --> 00:19:24,914
From who?

172
00:19:26,416 --> 00:19:28,168
We call them Engineers.

173
00:19:29,085 --> 00:19:30,587
- Engineers?
- Wow.

174
00:19:30,754 --> 00:19:31,588
Do you mind, um...

175
00:19:32,630 --> 00:19:34,424
...telling us what they engineered?

176
00:19:36,426 --> 00:19:38,094
They engineered us.

177
00:19:40,847 --> 00:19:41,681
Bullshit.

178
00:19:43,892 --> 00:19:45,769
Okay, so, uh, do you...

179
00:19:45,935 --> 00:19:47,604
...have anything to back that up?

180
00:19:48,271 --> 00:19:50,106
Look, if you're willing to discount...

181
00:19:50,273 --> 00:19:52,108
...centuries of Darwinism, that's...

182
00:19:53,401 --> 00:19:54,694
Whoo.

183
00:19:55,320 --> 00:19:56,279
But how do you know?

184
00:19:56,613 --> 00:19:59,115
- Hm?
- I don't.

185
00:19:59,616 --> 00:20:01,743
But it's what I choose to believe.

186
00:20:29,646 --> 00:20:30,688
Doctors.

187
00:20:31,773 --> 00:20:35,819
Miss Vickers would like to have a quick
word before the adventure begins.

188
00:20:43,284 --> 00:20:44,285
Wow, nice place.

189
00:20:44,452 --> 00:20:48,581
It's actually a separate module with its
own self-contained life support.

190
00:20:49,040 --> 00:20:50,708
Air, food.

191
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:54,587
Anything Miss Vickers would need
to survive a hostile environment.

192
00:20:54,838 --> 00:20:56,798
Okay, so she lives on a lifeboat.

193
00:20:56,965 --> 00:20:58,007
Yes.

194
00:20:58,174 --> 00:20:58,842
I do.

195
00:20:59,008 --> 00:21:01,261
I like to minimize risk.

196
00:21:01,845 --> 00:21:05,432
David, why don't you make
the doctors a drink?

197
00:21:06,015 --> 00:21:08,768
I'll take a vodka. Up.

198
00:21:09,185 --> 00:21:10,603
Charlie, look.

199
00:21:10,770 --> 00:21:13,148
It's a Pauling Med-Pod.

200
00:21:13,440 --> 00:21:15,817
They only made a dozen of these.

201
00:21:15,984 --> 00:21:16,818
Miss Shaw.

202
00:21:16,985 --> 00:21:19,446
<i>Please verbally state
the nature of your injury.</i>

203
00:21:19,863 --> 00:21:21,531
Please don't touch that.

204
00:21:21,698 --> 00:21:23,658
It's a very expensive piece of machinery.

205
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:26,703
It does bypass surgery.
What do you need it for?

206
00:21:28,538 --> 00:21:31,166
There might be some confusion
about our relationship.

207
00:21:32,250 --> 00:21:35,044
Weyland found you impressive
enough to fund this mission.

208
00:21:35,211 --> 00:21:37,714
But I'm fairly certain your
Engineers are scribblings...

209
00:21:37,881 --> 00:21:40,133
...of savages living in
dirty little caves.

210
00:21:41,342 --> 00:21:44,387
But let's say I'm wrong, and you
do find these beings down there...

211
00:21:45,555 --> 00:21:47,682
...you won't engage them...

212
00:21:47,974 --> 00:21:49,434
...you won't talk to them.

213
00:21:49,726 --> 00:21:52,353
You will do nothing
but report back to me.

214
00:21:55,231 --> 00:21:58,151
Um, Miss Vickers, is there an agenda that...

215
00:21:58,318 --> 00:21:59,944
...you're not telling us about?

216
00:22:00,570 --> 00:22:01,196
My company...

217
00:22:01,362 --> 00:22:03,907
...paid a trillion dollars to find
this place and bring you.

218
00:22:04,073 --> 00:22:06,868
Had you raised the monies
yourself, Mr. Holloway...

219
00:22:07,035 --> 00:22:09,913
...we'd happily be pursuing your agenda.

220
00:22:11,247 --> 00:22:12,499
But you didn't.

221
00:22:12,916 --> 00:22:14,501
And that makes you an employee.

222
00:22:15,960 --> 00:22:17,962
But if we can't make contact...

223
00:22:18,129 --> 00:22:20,632
...why did you even bring us here?

224
00:22:21,424 --> 00:22:24,177
Weyland was a superstitious man.

225
00:22:24,886 --> 00:22:27,263
He wanted a true believer on board.

226
00:22:29,933 --> 00:22:31,100
Cheers.

227
00:22:34,437 --> 00:22:35,605
So no response?

228
00:22:36,231 --> 00:22:37,357
I'm sorry, no.

229
00:22:37,524 --> 00:22:39,442
Maybe they didn't understand it.

230
00:22:40,818 --> 00:22:42,862
How are your lessons going, David?

231
00:22:44,072 --> 00:22:47,617
I spent two years deconstructing
dozens of ancient languages...

232
00:22:48,243 --> 00:22:49,869
...to their roots. I'm confident...

233
00:22:50,036 --> 00:22:51,538
...I can communicate with them...

234
00:22:51,788 --> 00:22:53,957
...provided your thesis is correct.

235
00:22:54,123 --> 00:22:57,377
Ha, ha. "Provided it's correct."
That's good.

236
00:22:57,544 --> 00:23:00,463
That's why they call it a thesis, doctor.

237
00:23:03,550 --> 00:23:05,260
What are you smiling about?

238
00:23:05,426 --> 00:23:07,303
Mr. Ravel, Mr. Chance, take her down.

239
00:23:07,470 --> 00:23:08,972
- Roger that.
- Yes, captain.

240
00:23:09,347 --> 00:23:10,807
Descent trajectory mapped.

241
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:12,517
How we doing?

242
00:23:12,684 --> 00:23:13,768
Great.

243
00:23:14,978 --> 00:23:15,812
All right, boss.

244
00:23:16,479 --> 00:23:17,272
All personnel...

245
00:23:17,438 --> 00:23:18,606
...this is the captain.

246
00:23:18,773 --> 00:23:20,191
Brace for entry.

247
00:23:20,358 --> 00:23:21,609
Yes, captain.

248
00:23:22,151 --> 00:23:23,653
That means you too, Vickers.

249
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:24,946
<i>All systems...</i>

250
00:23:25,113 --> 00:23:26,114
<i>...online.</i>

251
00:23:33,454 --> 00:23:34,706
What is the atmosphere?

252
00:23:34,872 --> 00:23:36,082
Atmosphere is...

253
00:23:36,249 --> 00:23:37,500
...71 percent nitrogen...

254
00:23:37,667 --> 00:23:38,835
...21 percent oxygen...

255
00:23:39,002 --> 00:23:40,128
...traces of argon gas.

256
00:23:40,336 --> 00:23:42,422
Whoa, now, that's weather.

257
00:23:42,589 --> 00:23:43,673
Just like home.

258
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:45,675
If you're breathing through
an exhaust pipe.

259
00:23:46,175 --> 00:23:48,136
CO2 is over 3 percent.

260
00:23:48,303 --> 00:23:50,346
Two minutes without a suit, you're dead.

261
00:23:53,850 --> 00:23:54,976
Peak portside.

262
00:23:55,226 --> 00:23:56,978
Whoa, 52,000!

263
00:23:57,228 --> 00:23:59,355
It makes Everest look
like a baby brother.

264
00:23:59,647 --> 00:24:01,858
All right, take us around.

265
00:24:02,025 --> 00:24:03,776
We'll use that as our point of entry.

266
00:24:06,195 --> 00:24:08,448
Terrain data rezzing up. We've got...

267
00:24:08,615 --> 00:24:10,033
...a couple of hard spots.

268
00:24:10,199 --> 00:24:11,701
Could be metal.

269
00:24:32,513 --> 00:24:33,723
No radio, no heat source.

270
00:24:34,807 --> 00:24:35,892
Nobody's home.

271
00:24:37,143 --> 00:24:38,728
There is nothing in the desert...

272
00:24:39,562 --> 00:24:41,230
...and no man needs nothing.

273
00:24:41,564 --> 00:24:42,815
What was that?

274
00:24:44,067 --> 00:24:46,152
Just something from a film I like.

275
00:24:50,490 --> 00:24:51,908
Let's go through that gateway.

276
00:24:52,200 --> 00:24:54,243
Reduce airspeed by a hundred knots.

277
00:24:58,873 --> 00:25:01,668
Going through. Nice and slow.

278
00:25:07,674 --> 00:25:08,758
Keep it steady, boys.

279
00:25:08,925 --> 00:25:09,592
There.

280
00:25:10,093 --> 00:25:11,761
No, what are you doing?

281
00:25:11,928 --> 00:25:14,806
Dr. Holloway, why don't you take a seat.

282
00:25:16,099 --> 00:25:18,059
Right there.
God does not build in...

283
00:25:18,226 --> 00:25:21,354
...straight lines.
Starboard side, this valley! Captain...

284
00:25:21,521 --> 00:25:23,022
...could you put us down there?

285
00:25:23,189 --> 00:25:25,441
I wouldn't be any good
if I couldn't do that.

286
00:25:25,608 --> 00:25:28,945
Mr. Ravel, starboard 90 degrees.

287
00:25:33,825 --> 00:25:37,036
- One mile, port bow.
- One mile, port bow.

288
00:25:37,328 --> 00:25:38,955
Engage landing sequence.

289
00:25:39,122 --> 00:25:40,248
Switch to manual.

290
00:25:40,415 --> 00:25:41,582
- <i>Commence landing.</i>
- Easy.

291
00:25:41,749 --> 00:25:43,793
- Roger that.
- Yeah, baby, yeah.

292
00:25:46,587 --> 00:25:47,964
Bringing her down in five.

293
00:25:48,131 --> 00:25:49,924
- Preparing RCS thrusters.
- Four.

294
00:25:53,594 --> 00:25:55,304
Two. Easy does it.

295
00:26:12,947 --> 00:26:15,658
<i>Generator systems are inactive.</i>

296
00:26:15,825 --> 00:26:18,202
<i>Temperature is at 2.724 K.</i>

297
00:26:30,047 --> 00:26:31,090
Captain, would you...

298
00:26:31,257 --> 00:26:33,259
...tell the survey team to suit up...

299
00:26:33,426 --> 00:26:34,844
...and meet us in the airlock.

300
00:26:35,386 --> 00:26:37,180
Only six hours left of daylight.

301
00:26:37,346 --> 00:26:39,015
- Leave it till morning.
- No, no, no.

302
00:26:39,682 --> 00:26:41,184
It's Christmas, captain...

303
00:26:41,350 --> 00:26:43,227
...and I want to open my presents.

304
00:26:43,519 --> 00:26:44,353
You, boy.

305
00:26:44,520 --> 00:26:45,855
You're coming with us.

306
00:26:46,189 --> 00:26:47,648
I'd be delighted.

307
00:26:53,196 --> 00:26:55,823
<i>Attention: Ramp will open
in five minutes.</i>

308
00:26:58,034 --> 00:26:59,619
Hey, Jackson...

309
00:26:59,952 --> 00:27:01,245
...what's that for?

310
00:27:01,996 --> 00:27:03,039
Expedition security.

311
00:27:03,206 --> 00:27:05,792
My job is to make sure
everybody is nice and safe.

312
00:27:07,376 --> 00:27:10,338
This is a scientific expedition.
No weapons.

313
00:27:10,505 --> 00:27:11,547
All right, then.

314
00:27:11,923 --> 00:27:13,299
Good luck with that.

315
00:27:16,219 --> 00:27:18,054
David, why are you wearing a suit, man?

316
00:27:19,555 --> 00:27:20,681
I beg your pardon?

317
00:27:20,848 --> 00:27:22,517
You don't breathe, remember?

318
00:27:22,683 --> 00:27:24,811
So why wear a suit?

319
00:27:25,394 --> 00:27:27,563
I was designed like this...

320
00:27:27,855 --> 00:27:31,943
...because you people are more comfortable
interacting with your own kind.

321
00:27:33,069 --> 00:27:36,405
If I didn't wear the suit,
it would defeat the purpose.

322
00:27:37,365 --> 00:27:39,325
They're making you guys pretty close, huh?

323
00:27:39,909 --> 00:27:41,369
Not too close, I hope.

324
00:27:51,212 --> 00:27:53,756
<i>Attention: Ramp will open in 15 seconds.</i>

325
00:27:59,095 --> 00:28:00,847
<i>Ramp will open in five...</i>

326
00:28:01,013 --> 00:28:05,101
<i>...four, three, two, one.</i>

327
00:28:22,451 --> 00:28:23,786
Hey.

328
00:28:24,453 --> 00:28:26,914
This is just one small step for mankind.

329
00:28:27,790 --> 00:28:28,791
Seriously?

330
00:28:33,671 --> 00:28:34,797
Whoo!

331
00:28:34,964 --> 00:28:36,757
You ready to do this? I know you are.

332
00:28:47,435 --> 00:28:48,394
Hey, Fifield.

333
00:28:48,561 --> 00:28:49,729
I want a spectrograph on this.

334
00:28:49,896 --> 00:28:52,148
I want to know if it's natural
or if somebody put it there.

335
00:28:52,315 --> 00:28:54,984
I can't tell you if it's natural or not.
But what I can...

336
00:28:55,151 --> 00:28:57,278
...tell you is, it's hollow.

337
00:29:21,427 --> 00:29:25,181
<i>Prometheus</i>, are you seeing this?

338
00:29:25,514 --> 00:29:26,682
Affirmative.

339
00:29:27,016 --> 00:29:28,309
We see it.

340
00:29:35,524 --> 00:29:36,192
Okay, ready?

341
00:29:36,359 --> 00:29:37,818
- Yeah.
- Let's do it.

342
00:30:11,268 --> 00:30:13,396
- After you.
- After you.

343
00:30:13,562 --> 00:30:14,522
Okay.

344
00:30:14,689 --> 00:30:15,731
Careful.

345
00:30:16,399 --> 00:30:18,401
Prometheus<i>, we're going in.</i>

346
00:30:18,567 --> 00:30:19,735
Copy that.

347
00:30:53,269 --> 00:30:54,103
What's back here?

348
00:30:54,270 --> 00:30:56,397
Some kind of a corridor?

349
00:30:57,273 --> 00:30:59,900
Mr. Fifield, let's get a grid of the structure.

350
00:31:00,067 --> 00:31:01,444
I want the whole interior.

351
00:31:02,445 --> 00:31:04,739
If there's anything in here
worth looking at...

352
00:31:04,905 --> 00:31:06,198
...these pups'll find them.

353
00:31:06,824 --> 00:31:07,783
"Pups"?

354
00:31:07,950 --> 00:31:10,286
Yeah, pups. My pups.

355
00:31:25,092 --> 00:31:27,845
<i>Prometheus</i>, we are now mapping.

356
00:31:32,141 --> 00:31:33,225
Copy that.

357
00:31:45,571 --> 00:31:46,572
Well, I'll be damned.

358
00:32:01,295 --> 00:32:02,546
Fifield, you got a read?

359
00:32:02,713 --> 00:32:03,839
Yeah. Um...

360
00:32:04,590 --> 00:32:07,510
Pups are saying this way.

361
00:32:26,112 --> 00:32:27,530
Look at this.

362
00:32:34,870 --> 00:32:36,497
Oh, Charlie.

363
00:32:40,501 --> 00:32:41,544
Jesus.

364
00:32:41,877 --> 00:32:43,712
The sunlight is heating the water.

365
00:32:44,380 --> 00:32:45,965
Check out the humidity.

366
00:32:46,132 --> 00:32:47,758
Yeah, and look at the CO2 levels.

367
00:32:48,259 --> 00:32:49,343
Outside...

368
00:32:49,510 --> 00:32:51,554
...it's completely toxic, and in here...

369
00:32:51,720 --> 00:32:53,806
...there's nothing. It's breathable.

370
00:32:55,891 --> 00:32:57,476
What are you doing?

371
00:32:57,643 --> 00:32:59,895
- Don't be an idiot.
- Don't be a skeptic.

372
00:33:00,396 --> 00:33:02,523
There's something
generating an atmosphere.

373
00:33:02,898 --> 00:33:04,150
David?

374
00:33:04,316 --> 00:33:06,485
- Dr. Holloway is correct.
- Cleaner than Earth...

375
00:33:06,652 --> 00:33:07,820
...actually.

376
00:33:07,987 --> 00:33:08,904
They were terraforming.

377
00:33:09,238 --> 00:33:10,698
Please don't do...

378
00:33:10,865 --> 00:33:12,658
I'm not wearing this thing anymore.

379
00:33:13,200 --> 00:33:14,493
Wish me luck, baby.

380
00:33:14,660 --> 00:33:17,246
<i>Do you copy?
Do not remove your headgear.</i>

381
00:33:30,426 --> 00:33:31,468
Whoo!

382
00:33:33,846 --> 00:33:35,764
You crazy bastard.

383
00:33:40,436 --> 00:33:41,687
Connect our suit cameras...

384
00:33:41,854 --> 00:33:46,108
...if you want to continue watching this
freak show. We are taking our helmets off.

385
00:33:47,234 --> 00:33:48,944
<i>Copy that. Switching feeds.</i>

386
00:33:50,946 --> 00:33:53,115
Well, come on. Pay up.

387
00:33:53,824 --> 00:33:56,535
- Pay what?
- What do you mean, "Pay what"?

388
00:33:56,702 --> 00:34:00,206
Something is manufacturing breathable
air down there. That, mate...

389
00:34:00,372 --> 00:34:03,125
- ...is terraforming.
- No, no. The bet was why we came here.

390
00:34:03,292 --> 00:34:06,170
If you said a dead old man wanted
to talk to Martians, I'd pay.

391
00:34:06,337 --> 00:34:07,713
Come on, a hundred credits.

392
00:34:07,880 --> 00:34:10,716
Put it towards a lap dance with Miss Vickers.
How about that, eh?

393
00:34:12,968 --> 00:34:15,137
It's minus 12 in here.

394
00:34:15,304 --> 00:34:17,431
So why is this water not frozen?

395
00:34:17,598 --> 00:34:19,016
Maybe it ain't water.

396
00:34:19,767 --> 00:34:21,227
Maybe it's Martian piss.

397
00:34:23,270 --> 00:34:26,315
That's your, um, scientific theory?

398
00:34:26,482 --> 00:34:28,400
Is it? Mr. Biology?

399
00:34:32,613 --> 00:34:34,490
Whatever it is, it sure is collagenous.

400
00:34:35,616 --> 00:34:37,326
Some kind of sediment, perhaps.

401
00:34:57,346 --> 00:34:58,514
Impressive.

402
00:35:18,200 --> 00:35:18,951
What was that?

403
00:35:19,868 --> 00:35:21,078
David?

404
00:35:21,954 --> 00:35:22,621
David?

405
00:35:24,039 --> 00:35:25,291
What is that?

406
00:35:27,293 --> 00:35:28,210
David?

407
00:35:36,885 --> 00:35:38,220
Ellie!

408
00:35:43,058 --> 00:35:43,892
What was that?

409
00:35:44,685 --> 00:35:46,562
I'm really glad we didn't bring weapons.

410
00:35:46,729 --> 00:35:49,023
- Come on, El. Let's go.
- Whose idea was that?

411
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:50,399
Keep up!

412
00:35:52,901 --> 00:35:53,485
Come on!

413
00:36:01,452 --> 00:36:02,786
Where did he go?

414
00:36:08,083 --> 00:36:09,668
Wait, wait, wait.

415
00:36:14,631 --> 00:36:18,093
There it is. There it is. There it is.

416
00:36:18,719 --> 00:36:21,221
Oh, my God, Charlie. We found them.

417
00:36:21,388 --> 00:36:22,931
What do you mean "them"?

418
00:36:24,224 --> 00:36:25,642
It is them, Ellie.

419
00:36:26,435 --> 00:36:28,020
It looks like a door...

420
00:36:28,395 --> 00:36:32,358
...and he's been decapitated by it.

421
00:36:33,609 --> 00:36:35,110
Son of a bitch.

422
00:36:36,862 --> 00:36:38,238
They were right.

423
00:36:41,742 --> 00:36:43,827
What did you want them to be? Wrong?

424
00:36:47,623 --> 00:36:48,749
David...

425
00:36:49,458 --> 00:36:51,126
...please tell me you can read that.

426
00:36:51,794 --> 00:36:53,045
Perhaps.

427
00:36:55,297 --> 00:36:56,965
No, I'm out of here.

428
00:36:58,967 --> 00:37:00,803
Hey, Fifield, where are you going?

429
00:37:02,471 --> 00:37:03,555
What?

430
00:37:05,307 --> 00:37:07,976
Look, I'm just a geologist.

431
00:37:08,143 --> 00:37:10,312
I like rocks. I love rocks!

432
00:37:10,771 --> 00:37:13,232
Now it's clear you two don't
give a shit about rocks.

433
00:37:13,399 --> 00:37:15,984
But what you do seem to care
about is gigantic dead...

434
00:37:16,151 --> 00:37:16,860
...bodies.

435
00:37:17,027 --> 00:37:20,072
As I don't really have anything
to contribute in the gigantic...

436
00:37:20,239 --> 00:37:21,657
...dead body arena...

437
00:37:23,158 --> 00:37:26,703
...I want to go back to the ship,
if you don't mind.

438
00:37:30,165 --> 00:37:30,916
Anyone wanna...

439
00:37:31,083 --> 00:37:33,210
...join me, hey? You staying?

440
00:37:33,377 --> 00:37:34,753
Uh...

441
00:37:34,920 --> 00:37:35,963
No, ship's good.

442
00:37:36,130 --> 00:37:38,340
Yeah. Ship very good.

443
00:37:39,299 --> 00:37:42,344
Congratulations on meeting your maker.

444
00:37:43,137 --> 00:37:44,346
Thank you.

445
00:37:45,013 --> 00:37:45,764
Pull yourself...

446
00:37:45,931 --> 00:37:46,849
...together, man.

447
00:37:50,310 --> 00:37:53,105
And I thought you were the crazy one.

448
00:38:01,155 --> 00:38:02,948
Do you have the carbon reader?

449
00:38:05,367 --> 00:38:06,201
Thank you.

450
00:38:12,875 --> 00:38:14,042
How long's it been dead?

451
00:38:16,795 --> 00:38:17,880
Two thousand years...

452
00:38:18,046 --> 00:38:19,214
...give or take.

453
00:38:22,885 --> 00:38:24,386
What are you doing, David?

454
00:38:25,345 --> 00:38:27,055
I'm attempting to open the door.

455
00:38:27,222 --> 00:38:29,516
Wait. We don't know
what's on the other side.

456
00:38:31,894 --> 00:38:33,312
Oops. Sorry.

457
00:38:37,983 --> 00:38:40,319
Look, Ford. It's the head.

458
00:38:50,787 --> 00:38:53,081
An amazing state of preservation.

459
00:38:53,373 --> 00:38:55,125
- We'll take it in.
- Yeah.

460
00:38:59,213 --> 00:39:00,756
Remarkably human.

461
00:39:18,440 --> 00:39:20,108
Beautiful painting.

462
00:39:21,235 --> 00:39:22,528
It's a mural.

463
00:39:35,123 --> 00:39:35,791
Stop.

464
00:39:35,958 --> 00:39:37,125
Stop. Don't touch it.

465
00:39:37,292 --> 00:39:38,418
Sorry.

466
00:39:39,545 --> 00:39:41,547
Please, don't touch anything.

467
00:39:42,464 --> 00:39:43,131
Sweating.

468
00:40:22,087 --> 00:40:23,297
Organic.

469
00:40:29,845 --> 00:40:30,846
Oh, no.

470
00:40:31,138 --> 00:40:33,849
Charlie, the murals are changing.

471
00:40:34,016 --> 00:40:36,435
I think we've affected
the atmosphere in the room.

472
00:40:36,602 --> 00:40:38,520
<i>Charlie, do you copy?</i>

473
00:40:39,521 --> 00:40:40,355
Oh, the head!

474
00:40:40,647 --> 00:40:42,733
Ford, quick, help me bag the head!

475
00:40:54,369 --> 00:40:55,871
- Boss.
- <i>What you got?</i>

476
00:40:56,872 --> 00:40:58,790
We got an incoming storm front.

477
00:40:58,957 --> 00:41:00,125
Silica...

478
00:41:00,292 --> 00:41:02,294
<i>...and lots of static. This is not good.</i>

479
00:41:02,461 --> 00:41:03,211
I see it.

480
00:41:07,299 --> 00:41:08,175
<i>Ground crew...</i>

481
00:41:08,342 --> 00:41:09,343
<i>...this is Janek.</i>

482
00:41:09,509 --> 00:41:11,345
<i>I need you to hustle back right now.</i>

483
00:41:11,637 --> 00:41:13,138
<i>Ground crew, do you copy me?</i>

484
00:41:13,305 --> 00:41:14,640
<i>I got 200-kilometer...</i>

485
00:41:14,806 --> 00:41:16,350
<i>...winds of airborne silica...</i>

486
00:41:16,516 --> 00:41:18,018
...and enough static to fry your suit.

487
00:41:18,185 --> 00:41:19,686
Copy that, sir.

488
00:41:19,853 --> 00:41:20,812
We need more time.

489
00:41:21,521 --> 00:41:22,856
I'll be closing the outer...

490
00:41:23,023 --> 00:41:24,232
...doors in 15 minutes.

491
00:41:24,399 --> 00:41:26,568
<i>I sincerely hope you can make it.</i>

492
00:41:29,321 --> 00:41:31,239
Charlie! David!

493
00:41:31,823 --> 00:41:32,908
We must leave now.

494
00:41:35,077 --> 00:41:36,745
This is just another tomb.

495
00:41:41,249 --> 00:41:42,459
David!

496
00:41:45,337 --> 00:41:47,214
- On three.
- Two, three, up.

497
00:41:47,381 --> 00:41:48,757
Let's go!

498
00:41:49,508 --> 00:41:51,259
Easy, easy, easy. Careful.

499
00:41:54,346 --> 00:41:57,057
David, we are leaving!

500
00:42:06,608 --> 00:42:07,442
Come on!

501
00:42:07,609 --> 00:42:09,319
Damn it, they've already taken off.

502
00:42:09,486 --> 00:42:10,445
Come on, let's go.

503
00:42:12,114 --> 00:42:15,242
Prometheus <i>to ground crew.
You're running out of time.</i>

504
00:42:15,409 --> 00:42:17,452
All right, come on. Let's go.

505
00:42:32,718 --> 00:42:34,136
Go faster!

506
00:42:57,993 --> 00:42:59,161
Charlie! The head!

507
00:43:00,120 --> 00:43:00,662
Ellie, no!

508
00:43:00,829 --> 00:43:01,496
What are you doing?

509
00:43:10,297 --> 00:43:11,089
<i>Hatch closure...</i>

510
00:43:11,256 --> 00:43:12,299
- <i>...in five seconds.</i>
- Shit.

511
00:43:13,550 --> 00:43:14,342
Damn it, hold on!

512
00:43:16,678 --> 00:43:17,471
Goddamn it.

513
00:43:17,637 --> 00:43:18,764
<i>Door closing.</i>

514
00:43:20,974 --> 00:43:22,058
Ellie, don't move!

515
00:43:26,396 --> 00:43:26,980
Aah, aah.

516
00:43:27,272 --> 00:43:27,939
Charlie!

517
00:43:31,485 --> 00:43:32,527
Grab my hand!

518
00:43:43,705 --> 00:43:45,081
- No!
- Aah!

519
00:43:45,248 --> 00:43:46,166
No, Ellie!

520
00:43:46,333 --> 00:43:47,125
Ellie!

521
00:44:25,121 --> 00:44:26,414
Unh. Got it.

522
00:44:34,381 --> 00:44:36,216
So, what was that, Ellie, huh?

523
00:44:36,508 --> 00:44:38,927
You could've compromised the mission.
Not to mention...

524
00:44:39,094 --> 00:44:40,554
...almost killing yourself!

525
00:44:40,720 --> 00:44:41,721
Are you all right?

526
00:44:42,097 --> 00:44:43,473
Yes.

527
00:44:43,765 --> 00:44:44,933
Thank you, David.

528
00:44:45,433 --> 00:44:46,935
My pleasure.

529
00:44:47,269 --> 00:44:49,104
Doctors, it's real good to have...

530
00:44:49,271 --> 00:44:51,565
...you back, but where's Millburn and Fifield?

531
00:44:51,731 --> 00:44:53,441
<i>Aren't they back yet?</i>

532
00:44:54,943 --> 00:44:56,444
- Get them up for me.
- All right.

533
